Gabriel benchmark comparison, 2026-10-24

The generated Alpha-derived interpreter was 1.522x faster than the direct C
interpreter by the geometric mean of all 29 workload time ratios. C took
52.2% more elapsed time, equivalent to 65.7% of Alpha's throughput on
this aggregate. Alpha was faster on every workload in this experiment.

The three matched boot pairs gave aggregate ratios of 1.523x, 1.501x, and
1.533x. The largest gaps were FFT (4.17x), TAKR (2.57x), and FRPOLY with
large integer coefficients (up to 2.17x). TAK had the smallest gap (1.12x).
Excluding FFT, the aggregate was 1.468x. The 17 non-FRPOLY workloads gave
1.503x; the 12 FRPOLY variants gave 1.549x. The overall result therefore does
not depend solely on FFT or the number of polynomial variants.

Times below are milliseconds per canonical suite invocation. For example,
the suite's FFT invocation performs 10 transforms. Each value is the median
of three boot medians; each boot median uses seven batched samples. Range
columns show the smallest and largest boot medians, not confidence intervals.
C/Alpha greater than 1 means the C interpreter takes longer.

