Host and builds

AMD Ryzen 7 PRO 7840U, Linux 7.2.11-200.fc44.x86_64, GCC 16.2.1.
Both executables used the repository's Release defaults: VLM_FAST=ON,
VLM_NATIVE=ON, -O3 -DNDEBUG -Ofast -march=native, with VLM_C_ENGINE toggled.
The C core retains its existing -fno-fast-math override for IEEE semantics.
No emulator code or build flags were changed for this experiment.

C executable: /home/nathan/dev/linux-vlm/build/src/genera
Alpha executable: /tmp/vlm-alpha-check/src/genera
Repository commit: a183dd8 (full revision and hashes in metadata.json).
"Alpha" denotes this repository's generated interpreter running as compiled
x86-64 code. This is not a comparison with native Alpha hardware.

Both used /home/nathan/dev/Genera-8-6.vlod, -vm 1024, the same TAP setup,
-spy no, and a private Xvfb display with GLX disabled. Genera and its helper
threads were pinned to logical CPU 6 at nice level 10. The CPU governor was
powersave, with boost enabled; frequencies were not locked and the CPU was
not reserved exclusively. Other host tasks could introduce timing noise.

Measurement protocol

The unmodified public-domain Symbolics Gabriel suite (revision 19) supplied
all 29 non-I/O workloads. suite.vbin and driver.vbin were compiled once on
the C interpreter before measurement, then reused unchanged by all six
fresh boots. World, executable, source, compiled-code, and batch-count hashes
were checked unchanged across the experiment and are recorded in metadata.
The run order was C1, Alpha1, Alpha2, C2, C3, Alpha3.

The custom driver uses fixed batches from ../../counts.json, one untimed
warm-up per workload, and seven measured batches per boot. There are
1218 measured batches in total, 21 per workload per engine. Batch durations
ranged from 0.102 to 1.185 seconds, comfortably above the roughly 1 ms timer
resolution. All samples, including early slow samples, are preserved.

Timing uses the original suite's TIMED-DURATION. Scheduling and oldspace
reclamation occur before timing; guest preemption is inhibited in the timed
batch. Ephemeral GC is enabled and dynamic GC disabled. The same batch-loop
and FUNCALL overhead is included for both interpreters. Compilation,
loading, warm-up, file writes, and screen updates are outside the intervals.

REAL is elapsed time, converted using Genera's declared 977 ticks/second.
RUN is also saved but is Genera's adjusted runtime, not Linux CPU time.
The comparison uses REAL. This protocol reports medians rather than the
original RUN-SERIES minimum-time statistic; these are Gabriel workload
measurements, not a reproduction of the original minimum-time score.

Boyer's first few samples were slower on both engines. Boot medians reduce
that effect without removing samples. Some boot medians varied more than
others, especially Alpha FFT and C CTAK; the full ranges are shown above.
The aggregate was consistent across all three pairs. These measurements
cover compute/memory workloads, not interactive responsiveness or I/O.

Validation and artifacts

Both builds passed their CTest suites before benchmarking (C: ivory-cpu;
Alpha: ivory-cpu and float-conversion). All 29 saved warm-up return values
matched byte-for-byte across both interpreters and all boots. This checks
returned results, not every internal side effect. All six runs reached the
completion marker and their host processes exited cleanly. No test Genera
instance remains running.

*-1/2/3.csv contain the raw samples; *.csv.values.gz contain the saved return
values; *.log and *-complete.txt record host output and guest completion.
summary.csv contains the derived comparison, and metadata.json records the
host, inputs, settings, and file hashes. Run from the repository root:

  python3 benchmarks/gabriel/analyze.py benchmarks/gabriel/results/2026-10-24

See ../../README for the build and guest-run reproduction procedure.
